This is a quick-and-dirty AI-assisted proof of concept for PyroWave, to test integration and performance. 

A more careful implementation will be done later if all goes well.

Based on initial work done by azafrob.


Native Metal on macOS
====================

CONFIG+=pyrowave now selects the upstream native Metal codec on macOS. Its output textures feed VTMetalRenderer through the existing FFmpegVideoDecoder decode-unit worker and FramePacer. Presentation, display-link timing, VRR policy, HDR metadata and the Metal dev UI therefore use the existing Metal path.

Decoding and rendering share an MTLDevice and use separate command queues. A shared event orders texture reads after decode without waiting on the CPU. An AVFrame retains the texture pool slot; both decode completion and render completion hold references, so dropping a queued frame cannot recycle textures still in use by the GPU.

Requirements: Apple Silicon for native PyroWave decoding, the normal Qt/macOS prebuilt dependencies, and the pyrowave-metal/pyrowave submodule. The universal binary still builds for Intel Macs, where conventional codecs remain available. Native PyroWave device probing fails on unsupported GPUs.

Build from the repository root with Qt's qmake on PATH:

    git submodule update --init --recursive
    python3 setup-deps.py
    mkdir -p build/macos-pyrowave
    qmake moonlight-qt.pro -o build/macos-pyrowave/Makefile CONFIG+=pyrowave \
        "QMAKE_APPLE_DEVICE_ARCHS=arm64 x86_64" QMAKE_MACOSX_DEPLOYMENT_TARGET=13.0
    make -C build/macos-pyrowave -j8 release

Use the existing PyroWave codec option in Settings or --video-codec PyroWave on the CLI. HDR and YUV444 choose the corresponding 10-bit and 4:4:4 profiles. CONFIG+=pyrowave-vulkan selects the existing MoltenVK/libplacebo implementation on macOS; its existing CMake/Granite build is still required. Linux CONFIG+=pyrowave continues using Vulkan. Builds without PyroWave are unchanged.

PyroWave's bitrate slider allows 450 Mbps normally and 1,500 Mbps with
"Unlock bitrate limit" enabled, three times the limits for other codecs.
The CLI uses the unlocked ceiling for its range warning; --bitrate is in Kbps.

For lower latency with fixed-sync Metal presentation, select Immediate pacing and
two frames in flight in the Metal dev UI. Three frames provide more headroom
for bursty arrivals. Immediate mode selects the newest
decoded frame after a backlog and presents at the next available vblank.
Display-locked pacing retains its scheduled cadence. Compare codecs with the same
resolution, frame rate, HDR, chroma and presentation settings; PyroWave's CPU
submission time alone excludes its asynchronous GPU decode work.

The native framing parser validates sequence geometry, block indices, record lengths and padding before passing data to the codec. Both legacy length-prefixed frames and record-framed whole frames are accepted. The protocol submodule stays at the branch's existing revision: this change does not negotiate newer record-framing features, partial RTP recovery, or a bitstream revision check. Parser support for missing-packet segments is exercised in isolation, but the production bridge currently receives only complete frames from that protocol revision.

Tests on an Apple Silicon Mac:

    mkdir -p build/tests-pyrowave
    qmake tests/pyrowave/pyrowave.pro -o build/tests-pyrowave/Makefile \
        QMAKE_APPLE_DEVICE_ARCHS=arm64 QMAKE_MACOSX_DEPLOYMENT_TARGET=13.0
    make -C build/tests-pyrowave -j8
    build/tests-pyrowave/tst_pyrowaveprofiles
    build/tests-pyrowave/tst_pyrowaveframing
    DYLD_LIBRARY_PATH="$PWD/libs/mac/lib" build/tests-pyrowave/tst_pyrowavemetal

    mkdir -p build/tests-framepacing
    qmake tests/framepacing/framequeue.pro -o build/tests-framepacing/Makefile
    make -C build/tests-framepacing -j8
    DYLD_LIBRARY_PATH="$PWD/libs/mac/lib" build/tests-framepacing/tst_framequeue

The frame-queue test checks newest-frame selection, FIFO behavior, ring wraparound,
retained renderer references and concurrent enqueue/selection ownership.

The GPU test exercises 4:2:0/4:4:4, 8/10-bit textures, both framings, non-block-aligned sizes, decoded pixel quality, malformed-frame recovery, texture pool exhaustion/reuse, cross-thread frame release, render event waits, and frames that outlive their decoder. Live streaming and external VRR testing are separate acceptance checks.
